1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,614
In sixteen weeks,

2
00:00:01,614 --> 00:00:03,207
at about ten hours a week,

3
00:00:03,207 --> 00:00:06,869
you can go from knowing nothing about the cloud to having shipped

4
00:00:06,869 --> 00:00:09,576
six projects that match what employers ask for.

5
00:00:09,576 --> 00:00:13,504
The notebook estimates it costs about one hundred fifty to two hundred

6
00:00:13,504 --> 00:00:15,203
fifty dollars out of pocket,

7
00:00:15,203 --> 00:00:16,902
if you tear everything down.

8
00:00:17,252 --> 00:00:19,739
And if you forget one G P U for a month,

9
00:00:19,739 --> 00:00:23,017
that single mistake can cost more than the whole path.

10
00:00:23,017 --> 00:00:25,165
This final lesson is the road map,

11
00:00:25,165 --> 00:00:25,956
the money,

12
00:00:25,956 --> 00:00:27,143
the certificates,

13
00:00:27,143 --> 00:00:30,196
and one last check on every number in this course.

14
00:00:31,446 --> 00:00:34,929
Welcome to lesson twelve of the cloud for A I engineers course,

15
00:00:34,929 --> 00:00:36,228
on The Code Notebook.

16
00:00:36,578 --> 00:00:39,713
The handwritten notes and the code for this lesson are linked in

17
00:00:39,713 --> 00:00:40,635
the description.

18
00:00:41,885 --> 00:00:43,770
You have seen the whole stack.

19
00:00:43,770 --> 00:00:44,989
Compute and chips,

20
00:00:44,989 --> 00:00:46,154
containers, data,

21
00:00:46,154 --> 00:00:47,373
training, serving,

22
00:00:47,373 --> 00:00:49,314
shipping, retrieval and agents,

23
00:00:49,314 --> 00:00:50,589
security, and cost.

24
00:00:50,589 --> 00:00:53,084
Each lesson ended with a lab you can run,

25
00:00:53,084 --> 00:00:56,577
and each number carried a label saying how it was produced.

26
00:00:56,927 --> 00:00:58,558
Now the question is order.

27
00:00:58,558 --> 00:01:00,080
What do you learn first,

28
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:01,276
what do you build,

29
00:01:01,276 --> 00:01:04,103
and how do you know you are done with each stop.

30
00:01:04,103 --> 00:01:05,027
Honest label.

31
00:01:05,027 --> 00:01:08,398
The order is my synthesis of what job postings ask for and

32
00:01:08,398 --> 00:01:10,409
which cost traps catch beginners.

33
00:01:10,409 --> 00:01:12,258
No source ranks it officially.

34
00:01:13,508 --> 00:01:15,141
The road has six stops.

35
00:01:15,141 --> 00:01:16,532
Cloud fundamentals,

36
00:01:16,532 --> 00:01:18,347
containers and Kubernetes,

37
00:01:18,347 --> 00:01:19,556
serving a model,

38
00:01:19,556 --> 00:01:22,097
retrieval and agents with evaluations,

39
00:01:22,097 --> 00:01:24,335
MLOps and infrastructure as code,

40
00:01:24,335 --> 00:01:26,573
and finally distributed training.

41
00:01:26,573 --> 00:01:28,629
Each stop uses the one before.

42
00:01:28,629 --> 00:01:33,045
You cannot put a model on Kubernetes without identity and networking.

43
00:01:33,395 --> 00:01:37,001
You cannot debug a slow retrieval app if you do not know

44
00:01:37,001 --> 00:01:38,383
what batching does.

45
00:01:38,383 --> 00:01:42,650
Distributed training costs the most and is the rarest ask for entry

46
00:01:42,650 --> 00:01:44,273
roles, so it goes last.

47
00:01:44,273 --> 00:01:45,595
The first letters,

48
00:01:45,595 --> 00:01:46,136
F, C,

49
00:01:46,136 --> 00:01:46,677
S, R,

50
00:01:46,677 --> 00:01:47,218
M, D,

51
00:01:47,218 --> 00:01:50,403
spell, Frugal Coders Spend Really Modest Dollars.

52
00:01:51,653 --> 00:01:52,529
One twist.

53
00:01:52,529 --> 00:01:53,656
Run stop four,

54
00:01:53,656 --> 00:01:56,035
retrieval, agents and evaluations,

55
00:01:56,035 --> 00:01:58,163
alongside stops two and three.

56
00:01:58,163 --> 00:02:00,354
It needs almost no cloud spend,

57
00:02:00,354 --> 00:02:01,857
mostly A P I tokens,

58
00:02:01,857 --> 00:02:05,300
and it maps to the most common ask in the postings.

59
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:08,931
Foundation models appear in fifty-one percent of them.

60
00:02:09,281 --> 00:02:11,578
So start reading for it in week five.

61
00:02:11,578 --> 00:02:13,540
Every stop ends with a project,

62
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:14,772
an estimated cost,

63
00:02:14,772 --> 00:02:16,229
and a done when check.

64
00:02:16,229 --> 00:02:17,686
Do not skip the check.

65
00:02:17,686 --> 00:02:21,216
Being able to show the thing working is what turns a course

66
00:02:21,216 --> 00:02:22,785
into portfolio evidence.

67
00:02:24,035 --> 00:02:24,782
Stop one,

68
00:02:24,782 --> 00:02:26,102
cloud fundamentals,

69
00:02:26,102 --> 00:02:27,366
weeks one and two.

70
00:02:27,366 --> 00:02:28,744
Learn in this order.

71
00:02:28,744 --> 00:02:30,696
Identity with least privilege,

72
00:02:30,696 --> 00:02:32,016
private networking,

73
00:02:32,016 --> 00:02:34,543
object storage for data and checkpoints,

74
00:02:34,543 --> 00:02:36,208
G P U compute and quotas,

75
00:02:36,208 --> 00:02:38,504
and billing with budgets and alerts.

76
00:02:38,504 --> 00:02:40,973
Learn deep learning basics in parallel.

77
00:02:41,323 --> 00:02:44,465
Project. Use Terraform to build a private network,

78
00:02:44,465 --> 00:02:46,036
a least privilege role,

79
00:02:46,036 --> 00:02:48,537
an encrypted bucket and a budget alert.

80
00:02:48,537 --> 00:02:50,690
Destroy it and rebuild from code.

81
00:02:50,690 --> 00:02:55,228
Done when destroy then apply rebuilds everything with zero console clicks,

82
00:02:55,228 --> 00:02:57,671
the role can read only its own bucket,

83
00:02:57,671 --> 00:02:59,998
and the budget alert actually fired.

84
00:03:01,248 --> 00:03:02,030
Stop two,

85
00:03:02,030 --> 00:03:03,834
containers and Kubernetes,

86
00:03:03,834 --> 00:03:05,277
weeks three to five.

87
00:03:05,277 --> 00:03:07,502
Dockerfiles for machine learning,

88
00:03:07,502 --> 00:03:09,185
registries, Deployments,

89
00:03:09,185 --> 00:03:10,929
Services and autoscaling,

90
00:03:10,929 --> 00:03:12,312
managed Kubernetes,

91
00:03:12,312 --> 00:03:14,958
and G P U node pools that scale to zero.

92
00:03:14,958 --> 00:03:17,784
Project. Containerise an inference service,

93
00:03:17,784 --> 00:03:20,971
deploy it on managed Kubernetes with autoscaling,

94
00:03:20,971 --> 00:03:22,234
and tear it down.

95
00:03:22,584 --> 00:03:26,114
Then Terraform a G P U node pool of one small card

96
00:03:26,114 --> 00:03:27,683
that scales to zero.

97
00:03:27,683 --> 00:03:31,018
Done when the node shows one allocatable G P U,

98
00:03:31,018 --> 00:03:33,045
a pod runs nvidia dash smi,

99
00:03:33,045 --> 00:03:36,445
and the pool drops back to zero nodes when idle.

100
00:03:36,445 --> 00:03:38,798
Request G P U quota in week one,

101
00:03:38,798 --> 00:03:41,218
because new accounts may need it.

102
00:03:42,468 --> 00:03:43,395
Stop three,

103
00:03:43,395 --> 00:03:44,631
serving a model,

104
00:03:44,631 --> 00:03:46,114
weeks six and seven.

105
00:03:46,114 --> 00:03:48,525
The servers postings name are vLLM,

106
00:03:48,525 --> 00:03:49,637
SGLang, T G I,

107
00:03:49,637 --> 00:03:51,182
Triton and Ray Serve.

108
00:03:51,182 --> 00:03:53,036
Learn time to first token,

109
00:03:53,036 --> 00:03:54,396
tokens per second,

110
00:03:54,396 --> 00:03:56,868
and ninety-fifth percentile latency,

111
00:03:56,868 --> 00:03:58,043
and the levers,

112
00:03:58,043 --> 00:03:59,959
batching, the memory cache,

113
00:03:59,959 --> 00:04:02,060
quantisation and G P U choice.

114
00:04:02,410 --> 00:04:06,625
Project. Serve a seven or eight billion parameter model on one small

115
00:04:06,625 --> 00:04:08,147
card and benchmark it.

116
00:04:08,147 --> 00:04:11,192
Done when you have a table of tokens per second,

117
00:04:11,192 --> 00:04:15,466
first token time and tail latency at three concurrency levels and two

118
00:04:15,466 --> 00:04:19,973
precisions, and you can explain why throughput and tail latency both rise

119
00:04:19,973 --> 00:04:21,203
with concurrency.

120
00:04:21,203 --> 00:04:23,193
Cost, a few dollars a session.

121
00:04:24,443 --> 00:04:25,303
Stop four,

122
00:04:25,303 --> 00:04:27,637
retrieval, agents and evaluations,

123
00:04:27,637 --> 00:04:29,050
weeks eight to ten.

124
00:04:29,050 --> 00:04:30,462
Build the pipeline,

125
00:04:30,462 --> 00:04:31,567
ingest, chunk,

126
00:04:31,567 --> 00:04:32,612
embed, store,

127
00:04:32,612 --> 00:04:34,024
retrieve, generate.

128
00:04:34,024 --> 00:04:36,051
Add agents with tool calling,

129
00:04:36,051 --> 00:04:37,279
an M C P server,

130
00:04:37,279 --> 00:04:38,262
and tracing.

131
00:04:38,262 --> 00:04:42,192
Wire evaluations of recall and faithfulness into your build.

132
00:04:42,542 --> 00:04:46,211
Done when your build goes red on a change that drops recall

133
00:04:46,211 --> 00:04:47,667
below your threshold,

134
00:04:47,667 --> 00:04:50,928
traces show every tool call with inputs and outputs,

135
00:04:50,928 --> 00:04:54,364
and you can say how big your evaluation set is and what

136
00:04:54,364 --> 00:04:55,645
it does not cover.

137
00:04:55,645 --> 00:04:57,043
Cost, mostly tokens,

138
00:04:57,043 --> 00:04:58,848
around zero to ten dollars.

139
00:05:00,098 --> 00:05:00,956
Stop five,

140
00:05:00,956 --> 00:05:02,672
MLOps and observability,

141
00:05:02,672 --> 00:05:04,450
weeks eleven to thirteen.

142
00:05:04,450 --> 00:05:05,921
Experiment tracking,

143
00:05:05,921 --> 00:05:07,208
a model registry,

144
00:05:07,208 --> 00:05:10,334
pipelines with an evaluation gate and a canary,

145
00:05:10,334 --> 00:05:12,724
Terraform for several environments,

146
00:05:12,724 --> 00:05:14,992
and monitoring with drift alerts.

147
00:05:14,992 --> 00:05:17,505
Fine tune with LoRA on spot capacity,

148
00:05:17,505 --> 00:05:20,017
saving checkpoints to object storage.

149
00:05:20,367 --> 00:05:23,807
Done when you kill the spot machine mid run and the job

150
00:05:23,807 --> 00:05:25,963
resumes from the last checkpoint,

151
00:05:25,963 --> 00:05:29,344
the registry shows a versioned model with its metrics,

152
00:05:29,344 --> 00:05:32,025
and a dashboard alert fires on fake drift.

153
00:05:32,025 --> 00:05:34,648
Cost, roughly two to twenty-five dollars.

154
00:05:35,898 --> 00:05:36,716
Stop six,

155
00:05:36,716 --> 00:05:38,287
distributed training,

156
00:05:38,287 --> 00:05:40,173
weeks fourteen to sixteen.

157
00:05:40,173 --> 00:05:42,813
Data, tensor and pipeline parallelism,

158
00:05:42,813 --> 00:05:44,133
ZeRO and F S D P,

159
00:05:44,133 --> 00:05:45,956
collective communication,

160
00:05:45,956 --> 00:05:50,230
and the memory and efficiency accounting you saw in lesson five.

161
00:05:50,230 --> 00:05:54,191
Start with basic data parallel on a free two card notebook.

162
00:05:54,541 --> 00:05:57,210
Then compare layouts on a few rented cards,

163
00:05:57,210 --> 00:05:59,652
and finish with the nanochat speed run.

164
00:05:59,652 --> 00:06:02,151
About two hours on eight H one hundreds,

165
00:06:02,151 --> 00:06:04,480
around forty-eight dollars on demand,

166
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:05,616
fifteen on spot.

167
00:06:05,616 --> 00:06:06,411
Done when,

168
00:06:06,411 --> 00:06:07,887
from model size alone,

169
00:06:07,887 --> 00:06:10,670
you can say whether plain data parallel fits,

170
00:06:10,670 --> 00:06:13,169
and your measured memory roughly agrees.

171
00:06:14,419 --> 00:06:15,898
How long does it take?

172
00:06:15,898 --> 00:06:19,084
The plan is sixteen weeks at about ten hours a week,

173
00:06:19,084 --> 00:06:20,791
a hundred and sixty hours.

174
00:06:20,791 --> 00:06:22,497
That ten is my assumption.

175
00:06:22,497 --> 00:06:25,228
At five hours a week it is thirty-two weeks.

176
00:06:25,228 --> 00:06:26,081
At fifteen,

177
00:06:26,081 --> 00:06:27,219
ten point seven.

178
00:06:27,219 --> 00:06:28,015
At twenty,

179
00:06:28,015 --> 00:06:28,584
eight.

180
00:06:28,934 --> 00:06:32,315
There is also a twelve week fast track that skips or merges

181
00:06:32,315 --> 00:06:33,496
four of the weeks,

182
00:06:33,496 --> 00:06:34,891
the quantisation week,

183
00:06:34,891 --> 00:06:35,965
the agents week,

184
00:06:35,965 --> 00:06:38,702
the observability week and the efficiency week.

185
00:06:38,702 --> 00:06:41,815
Change the hours in the script and read your own date.

186
00:06:43,065 --> 00:06:44,260
What does it cost?

187
00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:48,118
The notebook's own estimate for the whole path is about one hundred

188
00:06:48,118 --> 00:06:50,237
fifty to two hundred fifty dollars,

189
00:06:50,237 --> 00:06:51,975
if you tear everything down.

190
00:06:51,975 --> 00:06:53,985
I added up the project estimates,

191
00:06:53,985 --> 00:06:57,354
and got thirty-two to a hundred fifty-five before credits.

192
00:06:57,354 --> 00:06:59,093
So budget the higher figure.

193
00:06:59,443 --> 00:07:01,421
Spend climbs along the road.

194
00:07:01,421 --> 00:07:02,782
Zero for stop one,

195
00:07:02,782 --> 00:07:05,255
a few dollars a lab for two to five,

196
00:07:05,255 --> 00:07:06,615
tens for stop six.

197
00:07:06,615 --> 00:07:09,521
Pay for G P U time only from stop three on.

198
00:07:09,521 --> 00:07:10,758
Free tiers help.

199
00:07:10,758 --> 00:07:12,551
New accounts get credits,

200
00:07:12,551 --> 00:07:16,137
and Kaggle gives about thirty free G P U hours a week.

201
00:07:17,387 --> 00:07:18,619
Now the mistake.

202
00:07:18,619 --> 00:07:21,637
A single small G P U node left up for a month

203
00:07:21,637 --> 00:07:25,826
is seven hundred thirty hours at fifty cents to a dollar twenty,

204
00:07:25,826 --> 00:07:29,892
three hundred sixty-five to eight hundred seventy-six dollars.

205
00:07:29,892 --> 00:07:33,403
That is two to six times the cost of the entire path.

206
00:07:33,403 --> 00:07:36,791
Idle resources are the number one way to overspend.

207
00:07:37,141 --> 00:07:38,687
So the habit is simple.

208
00:07:38,687 --> 00:07:39,832
After every lab,

209
00:07:39,832 --> 00:07:41,435
run the destroy command,

210
00:07:41,435 --> 00:07:44,125
then list your clusters and expect nothing.

211
00:07:44,125 --> 00:07:45,099
Every Friday,

212
00:07:45,099 --> 00:07:46,644
check the billing page.

213
00:07:46,644 --> 00:07:47,961
Habit, not heroics.

214
00:07:49,211 --> 00:07:50,989
Other traps worth knowing.

215
00:07:50,989 --> 00:07:54,902
Free networking labs that include a managed N A T gateway keep

216
00:07:54,902 --> 00:07:56,324
billing by the hour,

217
00:07:56,324 --> 00:07:58,814
so avoid them or destroy the same day.

218
00:07:58,814 --> 00:08:02,133
An old Kubernetes version on A W S can move from ten

219
00:08:02,133 --> 00:08:05,749
cents to about sixty cents an hour for the control plane,

220
00:08:05,749 --> 00:08:07,468
so keep versions current.

221
00:08:07,818 --> 00:08:08,661
On Google,

222
00:08:08,661 --> 00:08:13,175
the monthly management credit covers zonal and Autopilot clusters only,

223
00:08:13,175 --> 00:08:15,763
not regional clusters or machine costs.

224
00:08:15,763 --> 00:08:20,037
And spot machines with no checkpoints can cost more than on demand,

225
00:08:20,037 --> 00:08:21,541
as lesson two showed.

226
00:08:21,541 --> 00:08:22,625
Read the bill,

227
00:08:22,625 --> 00:08:23,949
not just the plan.

228
00:08:25,199 --> 00:08:27,018
Which G P U for which stop?

229
00:08:27,018 --> 00:08:30,305
Free notebooks for stop one and basic data parallel.

230
00:08:30,305 --> 00:08:32,535
A small twenty-four gigabyte card,

231
00:08:32,535 --> 00:08:35,528
roughly fifty cents to a dollar twenty an hour,

232
00:08:35,528 --> 00:08:37,171
for stops two and three.

233
00:08:37,171 --> 00:08:41,045
A mid size card around ninety-two cents for bigger benchmarks.

234
00:08:41,045 --> 00:08:42,277
An A one hundred,

235
00:08:42,277 --> 00:08:43,744
about a dollar fifty,

236
00:08:43,744 --> 00:08:44,800
for stop five.

237
00:08:45,150 --> 00:08:46,903
And rented H one hundreds,

238
00:08:46,903 --> 00:08:50,699
about a dollar thirty-three to three dollars on marketplaces,

239
00:08:50,699 --> 00:08:51,692
for stop six.

240
00:08:51,692 --> 00:08:56,248
Prices are vendor and aggregator figures from September twenty twenty-six,

241
00:08:56,248 --> 00:08:57,299
and they move.

242
00:08:57,299 --> 00:09:01,037
Spend credits on the central processor and Kubernetes stops,

243
00:09:01,037 --> 00:09:03,782
and rent marketplace cards for stops three,

244
00:09:03,782 --> 00:09:04,775
five and six.

245
00:09:06,025 --> 00:09:09,862
Certificates. In twenty twenty-six there was big churn.

246
00:09:09,862 --> 00:09:13,959
The A W S Machine Learning Specialty exam retired in March,

247
00:09:13,959 --> 00:09:16,560
the Azure A I Engineer exam in June,

248
00:09:16,560 --> 00:09:19,551
and the Azure Data Scientist exam in June.

249
00:09:19,551 --> 00:09:20,722
A mnemonic is,

250
00:09:20,722 --> 00:09:21,958
who went M A D.

251
00:09:21,958 --> 00:09:22,608
M L S,

252
00:09:22,608 --> 00:09:23,843
A I one oh two,

253
00:09:23,843 --> 00:09:25,144
D P one hundred.

254
00:09:25,494 --> 00:09:29,164
Swaps. The A W S ML Engineer exam moved to version C

255
00:09:29,164 --> 00:09:30,016
zero two.

256
00:09:30,016 --> 00:09:33,423
The Azure engineer exam became A I one oh three,

257
00:09:33,423 --> 00:09:34,406
a new exam,

258
00:09:34,406 --> 00:09:37,683
and Azure A I Fundamentals became nine oh one.

259
00:09:37,683 --> 00:09:39,452
If you hold an old one,

260
00:09:39,452 --> 00:09:42,204
it stays active for its normal period.

261
00:09:42,204 --> 00:09:46,988
Always check the exam code on the official page right before booking.

262
00:09:48,238 --> 00:09:49,884
Which certificate when?

263
00:09:49,884 --> 00:09:50,676
Stop one,

264
00:09:50,676 --> 00:09:52,139
a fundamentals exam,

265
00:09:52,139 --> 00:09:55,735
like the A W S A I Practitioner at one hundred dollars.

266
00:09:55,735 --> 00:09:56,527
Stop two,

267
00:09:56,527 --> 00:09:59,574
the Kubernetes associate at two hundred fifty,

268
00:09:59,574 --> 00:10:04,085
then the hands on Kubernetes administrator at four hundred forty-five.

269
00:10:04,085 --> 00:10:04,938
Stop four,

270
00:10:04,938 --> 00:10:09,144
the A W S Generative A I Developer Professional at three hundred.

271
00:10:09,494 --> 00:10:10,335
Stop five,

272
00:10:10,335 --> 00:10:13,640
the Google Professional ML Engineer at two hundred,

273
00:10:13,640 --> 00:10:15,383
or the A W S ML Engineer.

274
00:10:15,383 --> 00:10:19,470
Hands on exams in a live terminal are probably a stronger signal

275
00:10:19,470 --> 00:10:20,972
than multiple choice,

276
00:10:20,972 --> 00:10:23,376
though that is inferred from format,

277
00:10:23,376 --> 00:10:25,299
not from an employer survey.

278
00:10:25,299 --> 00:10:28,965
No data shows how often postings require a specific cert.

279
00:10:28,965 --> 00:10:30,468
Projects beat badges.

280
00:10:30,468 --> 00:10:33,112
Use certs as structure and a tiebreaker.

281
00:10:34,362 --> 00:10:35,927
What to read and watch.

282
00:10:35,927 --> 00:10:37,608
If you are short on time,

283
00:10:37,608 --> 00:10:38,767
a minimal stack.

284
00:10:38,767 --> 00:10:41,955
Karpathy's Zero to Hero for from scratch intuition.

285
00:10:41,955 --> 00:10:44,158
Made With ML to learn how to ship.

286
00:10:44,158 --> 00:10:45,839
The A I Engineering book,

287
00:10:45,839 --> 00:10:48,157
plus the Hugging Face agents course,

288
00:10:48,157 --> 00:10:49,896
for foundation model apps.

289
00:10:49,896 --> 00:10:53,896
Then the Ultra Scale Playbook and nanochat for training at scale.

290
00:10:54,246 --> 00:10:56,480
Free options include fast dot A I,

291
00:10:56,480 --> 00:10:59,654
the Hugging Face language model and M C P courses,

292
00:10:59,654 --> 00:11:03,005
and Stanford's language models from scratch lectures.

293
00:11:03,005 --> 00:11:06,709
The notebook marks anything it could not verify this round,

294
00:11:06,709 --> 00:11:08,825
such as current course catalogs,

295
00:11:08,825 --> 00:11:10,471
so check before you buy.

296
00:11:11,721 --> 00:11:13,516
What do employers ask for?

297
00:11:13,516 --> 00:11:18,003
In a sample of forty-three thousand four hundred eighty United States A

298
00:11:18,003 --> 00:11:21,473
I engineering postings through July twenty twenty-six,

299
00:11:21,473 --> 00:11:23,927
Python appeared in sixty-two percent,

300
00:11:23,927 --> 00:11:25,961
cloud platforms in fifty-five,

301
00:11:25,961 --> 00:11:28,055
foundation models in fifty-one.

302
00:11:28,055 --> 00:11:30,628
About fifteen hundred new roles a week.

303
00:11:30,978 --> 00:11:33,667
Platform and L L M ops roles add vLLM,

304
00:11:33,667 --> 00:11:35,396
Kubernetes with G P Us,

305
00:11:35,396 --> 00:11:37,445
Terraform and observability,

306
00:11:37,445 --> 00:11:40,134
and usually want three to seven years.

307
00:11:40,134 --> 00:11:42,247
If you are switching careers,

308
00:11:42,247 --> 00:11:45,897
the realistic way in is an applied A I engineer role,

309
00:11:45,897 --> 00:11:47,306
stops one to four,

310
00:11:47,306 --> 00:11:49,995
with platform skills added on the job.

311
00:11:51,245 --> 00:11:52,755
A practical trick.

312
00:11:52,755 --> 00:11:56,048
The projects here map onto posting language.

313
00:11:56,048 --> 00:11:58,107
vLLM throughput benchmark.

314
00:11:58,107 --> 00:12:00,165
Terraform G P U node pool.

315
00:12:00,165 --> 00:12:02,360
RAG evaluation suite in C I.

316
00:12:02,360 --> 00:12:04,830
LoRA on spot with checkpointing.

317
00:12:04,830 --> 00:12:09,565
Put those exact phrases in your repository read me files and your

318
00:12:09,565 --> 00:12:10,319
resume.

319
00:12:10,669 --> 00:12:12,142
Show the thing working,

320
00:12:12,142 --> 00:12:13,234
with the number.

321
00:12:13,234 --> 00:12:16,507
A table of tokens per second you measured beats a badge.

322
00:12:16,507 --> 00:12:19,781
A repository that says what it cost and what it does not

323
00:12:19,781 --> 00:12:20,981
cover earns trust.

324
00:12:20,981 --> 00:12:24,201
That is the habit this whole course has tried to build.

325
00:12:25,451 --> 00:12:27,953
A few honest limits before you start.

326
00:12:27,953 --> 00:12:31,616
Prices, exam codes and free credit rules change monthly,

327
00:12:31,616 --> 00:12:35,156
so treat every dollar figure here as a dated estimate.

328
00:12:35,156 --> 00:12:38,880
Sources disagree on several certificate prices and dates,

329
00:12:38,880 --> 00:12:40,833
and the notebook flags them.

330
00:12:41,183 --> 00:12:45,083
Free trial G P U quota was not rechecked in twenty twenty-six,

331
00:12:45,083 --> 00:12:47,447
so do not plan stop three around it.

332
00:12:47,447 --> 00:12:50,992
And the career numbers come from public posting samples,

333
00:12:50,992 --> 00:12:52,528
which are directional,

334
00:12:52,528 --> 00:12:53,651
not guarantees.

335
00:12:53,651 --> 00:12:57,019
Verify the one or two that decide your next purchase.

336
00:12:58,269 --> 00:12:59,191
One last lab,

337
00:12:59,191 --> 00:13:00,872
and it is a different kind.

338
00:13:00,872 --> 00:13:02,336
I made a claims ledger.

339
00:13:02,336 --> 00:13:05,752
For thirty-two headline numbers from lessons one to eleven,

340
00:13:05,752 --> 00:13:08,951
a script looks the number up in the saved output of the

341
00:13:08,951 --> 00:13:10,307
lab that produced it,

342
00:13:10,307 --> 00:13:11,662
and prints its label.

343
00:13:11,662 --> 00:13:13,289
All thirty-two were found.

344
00:13:13,639 --> 00:13:15,936
Eleven were measured on one machine,

345
00:13:15,936 --> 00:13:19,093
thirteen came from simulations with assumed inputs,

346
00:13:19,093 --> 00:13:20,529
five were arithmetic,

347
00:13:20,529 --> 00:13:22,481
and three used synthetic data.

348
00:13:22,481 --> 00:13:25,065
It cannot prove the experiments are good.

349
00:13:25,065 --> 00:13:28,280
It proves each claim came from a file you can rerun,

350
00:13:28,280 --> 00:13:30,979
and that is the standard to hold anyone to,

351
00:13:30,979 --> 00:13:31,955
including me.

352
00:13:33,205 --> 00:13:35,155
A few things the labs showed.

353
00:13:35,155 --> 00:13:39,412
A seventy billion model at eight bit plus thirty-two chats needs one

354
00:13:39,412 --> 00:13:41,481
hundred eighty-seven gigabytes.

355
00:13:41,481 --> 00:13:45,737
Skipping checkpoints on spot turned a hundred hour job into over two

356
00:13:45,737 --> 00:13:47,629
million hours in simulation.

357
00:13:47,629 --> 00:13:52,180
Twenty thousand small files read twenty-five times slower than one shard.

358
00:13:52,530 --> 00:13:56,521
Mixed precision training state measured sixteen bytes per parameter.

359
00:13:56,521 --> 00:13:59,680
The memory cache made generation twelve times faster.

360
00:13:59,680 --> 00:14:02,894
An eval gate blocked a change that raised the average.

361
00:14:02,894 --> 00:14:06,164
And the keyword filter stopped three of twelve attacks,

362
00:14:06,164 --> 00:14:08,492
where an egress allowlist leaked none.

363
00:14:09,742 --> 00:14:11,104
Our course project,

364
00:14:11,104 --> 00:14:12,347
ShopNest Copilot,

365
00:14:12,347 --> 00:14:13,709
is now a full plan.

366
00:14:13,709 --> 00:14:15,071
It fits its memory,

367
00:14:15,071 --> 00:14:17,320
runs on the right mix of capacity,

368
00:14:17,320 --> 00:14:18,978
ships as layered images,

369
00:14:18,978 --> 00:14:20,340
reads sharded data,

370
00:14:20,340 --> 00:14:22,767
and serves with a cache and batching.

371
00:14:22,767 --> 00:14:25,669
It deploys through an eval gate and a canary.

372
00:14:26,019 --> 00:14:28,548
It retrieves with chunks that were tested,

373
00:14:28,548 --> 00:14:30,308
agents that survive a crash,

374
00:14:30,308 --> 00:14:34,268
and an architecture that limits what an injected instruction can do.

375
00:14:34,268 --> 00:14:36,523
And it is priced per resolved ticket.

376
00:14:36,523 --> 00:14:38,998
That is what a cloud A I engineer builds.

377
00:14:38,998 --> 00:14:40,372
Not any single trick,

378
00:14:40,372 --> 00:14:41,472
but all of them,

379
00:14:41,472 --> 00:14:42,737
in the right order.

380
00:14:43,987 --> 00:14:45,680
What do you do this week?

381
00:14:45,680 --> 00:14:49,823
Open one cloud account and set a budget alert before anything else.

382
00:14:49,823 --> 00:14:51,457
Request G P U quota now,

383
00:14:51,457 --> 00:14:53,383
since approval can take days.

384
00:14:53,383 --> 00:14:55,134
Pick one stop one project,

385
00:14:55,134 --> 00:14:56,651
the Terraform network,

386
00:14:56,651 --> 00:14:58,635
role, bucket and budget alert.

387
00:14:58,985 --> 00:15:01,152
Run the lab scripts from this course,

388
00:15:01,152 --> 00:15:02,632
change a number in each,

389
00:15:02,632 --> 00:15:04,323
and write down what you saw.

390
00:15:04,323 --> 00:15:05,327
Then on Friday,

391
00:15:05,327 --> 00:15:07,916
run the teardown commands and check the bill.

392
00:15:07,916 --> 00:15:09,026
That is week one.

393
00:15:09,026 --> 00:15:10,135
Sixteen of those,

394
00:15:10,135 --> 00:15:12,091
and you are a cloud A I engineer.

395
00:15:13,341 --> 00:15:14,568
Interview corner.

396
00:15:14,568 --> 00:15:19,125
Asked why you run retrieval and agents in parallel with the infrastructure

397
00:15:19,125 --> 00:15:20,002
stops, say:

398
00:15:20,002 --> 00:15:22,047
it needs almost no cloud spend,

399
00:15:22,047 --> 00:15:25,143
and foundation models appear in half of postings.

400
00:15:25,143 --> 00:15:28,299
Asked how a spot run can cost more than on demand,

401
00:15:28,299 --> 00:15:29,993
say: with no checkpoints,

402
00:15:29,993 --> 00:15:31,804
an eviction loses all work.

403
00:15:32,154 --> 00:15:34,435
Asked how to avoid a surprise bill,

404
00:15:34,435 --> 00:15:37,301
say: set a budget alert before anything else,

405
00:15:37,301 --> 00:15:39,055
tear down after every lab,

406
00:15:39,055 --> 00:15:41,629
and check for idle resources every week.

407
00:15:41,629 --> 00:15:44,787
One forgotten G P U can cost more than the rest of

408
00:15:44,787 --> 00:15:46,073
the path combined.

409
00:15:47,323 --> 00:15:48,865
Four last words to keep.

410
00:15:48,865 --> 00:15:50,903
Teardown: destroy what you built,

411
00:15:50,903 --> 00:15:53,161
then confirm nothing is left billing.

412
00:15:53,161 --> 00:15:53,932
Done when:

413
00:15:53,932 --> 00:15:56,025
a check that proves a stage works.

414
00:15:56,375 --> 00:15:58,916
Portfolio: a project you can show running,

415
00:15:58,916 --> 00:16:00,076
with its numbers.

416
00:16:00,076 --> 00:16:00,850
And label:

417
00:16:00,850 --> 00:16:03,778
the honest note on whether a number was measured,

418
00:16:03,778 --> 00:16:05,214
simulated, calculated,

419
00:16:05,214 --> 00:16:06,043
or made up.

420
00:16:07,293 --> 00:16:08,647
Recap of the road.

421
00:16:08,647 --> 00:16:09,509
Six stops,

422
00:16:09,509 --> 00:16:12,340
frugal coders spend really modest dollars.

423
00:16:12,340 --> 00:16:14,064
Run retrieval alongside.

424
00:16:14,064 --> 00:16:16,403
Sixteen weeks at ten hours a week,

425
00:16:16,403 --> 00:16:20,466
about a hundred fifty to two hundred fifty dollars if you tear

426
00:16:20,466 --> 00:16:23,729
down. A forgotten G P U costs more than the path.

427
00:16:24,079 --> 00:16:26,358
Check the exam code before you book.

428
00:16:26,358 --> 00:16:27,783
Projects beat badges.

429
00:16:27,783 --> 00:16:29,891
And label every number you quote.

430
00:16:29,891 --> 00:16:31,886
Thank you for learning with me.

